 "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cm4gjjwvvlzro": {
  "summary": {
   "sections": [
    {
     "heading": "What happened",
     "text": "Pope Leo XIV travelled down the Champs-Élysées in his popemobile before a huge open-air Mass in central Paris. Hundreds of thousands of people gathered to greet him, with over half a million expected at Place de la Concorde. Earlier, the Pope visited a refugee support centre and met migrants. French President Emmanuel Macron met the Pope on Friday but is avoiding the Masses to respect state secularism rules. However, his wife, the prime minister, and several presidential candidates attended."
    },
    {
     "heading": "Why it matters",
     "text": "The Church believes the large crowds show growing spirituality in France and Europe. During his visit, the Pope urged dignity and peace, praised the role of women, and warned against losing humanity to artificial intelligence."
    },
    {
     "heading": "What happens next",
     "text": "On Sunday, 150,000 people will attend Mass with the Pope in Lourdes. On Monday, he will visit Metz to honour Robert Schuman, a founding father of the European Union."
    }
   ],
